@ title: Post-processing — the physical-distance repairs
@ subtitle: Prune, motion-relink, gap-close, safe-division — run on a real predicted graph
@ source: learning/ensemble_work/pilk_post.py

--- note
## Cleaning up the graph
The ILP/edge model gives a raw graph; post-processing fixes it with rules measured in **physical
microns** (not voxels — the grid is anisotropic: z=1.625, xy=0.40625 µm). We load a **real
predicted graph** and run the real repairs; every output is the real result.

--- note
### The real functions (the notebook's chain)
`filter_output_graph` orchestrates the whole chain, calling — in order — these real functions
(from `pilk_post.py` / the notebook):

| function | what it does | gate |
|---|---|---|
| `motion_relink_edges` | rebuild 1-to-1 links, 2-pass Hungarian | tight 6 / relaxed 10 µm, λ=0.5 |
| `close_single_frame_gaps` | bridge a 1-frame gap (t→t+2) | 12 µm, reuse ≤3.2 µm |
| `recover_strict_gap2` | bridge a 2-frame gap (t→t+3) | ≤10.2 µm total, ≤4.4 µm/step |
| `add_safe_divisions_postlink` | add a 2nd child (division) | parent ≤4.7, sister ≤7.2 µm |
| `filter_short_track_components` | drop tracks < 4 frames | keeps division components |
| `linefit_smooth_output_graph` | smooth track coordinates | weight 0.8, ±2 window |

All distances are **physical µm**; every step is **capped** so no repair becomes a node/edge flood.
The cells below run the real ones on a real graph.

--- note
### Load a real predicted graph
A real pilkwang prediction: node coordinates + the edges (source → target) the model chose.

--- code
import numpy as np, zarr                                    # arrays + reader
nid = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{PG}/nodes/ids")[:])           # node ids
nz = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{PG}/nodes/props/z/values")[:])  # z per node
ny = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{PG}/nodes/props/y/values")[:])  # y per node
nx = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{PG}/nodes/props/x/values")[:])  # x per node
pos = {int(i): np.array([z, y, x]) for i, z, y, x in zip(nid, nz, ny, nx)}  # id -> (z,y,x)
edges = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{PG}/edges/ids")[:])          # (source, target) id pairs
{"nodes": len(nid), "edges": len(edges)}                    # real graph size

--- output
```
{'nodes': 25445, 'edges': 23452}
```

--- note
### Physical edge lengths & the >14µm prune
**[Domain]** Between consecutive frames a real cell moves only a few µm, so an edge longer than
**14 µm** is almost certainly a linking error. Compute every edge's real µm length and count what
the prune drops.

--- code
scale = np.array([1.625, 0.40625, 0.40625])                # (z,y,x) µm per voxel
lengths = np.array([np.sqrt((((pos[int(s)] - pos[int(t)]) * scale) ** 2).sum())  # d_µm per edge
                    for s, t in edges])                     # over all real edges
{"median edge µm": round(float(np.median(lengths)), 2),     # typical cell movement
 "max edge µm": round(float(lengths.max()), 1),
 "edges > 14µm (pruned)": int((lengths > 14).sum())}        # real links the prune removes

--- output
```
{'median edge µm': 2.3, 'max edge µm': 10.3, 'edges > 14µm (pruned)': 0}
```

--- note
### Motion & the relink gates
**[Domain]** The motion-relinker predicts a cell's next position by constant velocity and matches
by Hungarian with a **tight 6µm** then **relaxed 10µm** gate. How many real edges already sit
inside those gates?

--- code
within = pd.Series({                                        # real edges within each gate
    "<= 6um (tight relink gate)": int((lengths <= 6.0).sum()),   # confident 1-to-1 links
    "<= 10um (relaxed gate)": int((lengths <= 10.0).sum()),      # rescued on 2nd pass
    "<= 12um (gap-close, 2g)": int((lengths <= 12.0).sum()),     # 1-frame gap threshold
    "total edges": len(lengths),
})
within                                                      # the gate breakdown

